{
  "image_name": "f850e9267d14a58fc9cdcb24577d4344.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes key operating limits and device components for electrochemical CO2 reduction in a membrane-based electrolyser. The upper part shows that performance depends on balancing CO2 flow, water content, and pH. If the CO2 flow is too low, product formation is limited and hydrogen evolution competes; if it is too high, single-pass conversion drops because much of the CO2 exits unreacted. Water concentration also has an optimum: too little water appears to dehydrate the catalyst layer, lowering faradaic efficiency and increasing voltage, while too much causes flooding and suppresses CO2 reduction. pH is likewise critical: low pH favors hydrogen evolution, whereas high pH drives CO2 into bicarbonate or carbonate rather than desired reduction products.\n\nThe lower part is an exploded view of a PEM-based CO2 electrolyser, showing anode and cathode housings, flow plates, membrane, and a gas diffusion electrode. CO2 enters the cathode side and products such as CO leave from that side, while electrolyte and oxygen are associated with the anode side. The diagram highlights that both the polymer electrolyte membrane and the gas diffusion electrode strongly influence catalysis through properties such as conductivity, thickness, water uptake, morphology, porosity, and hydrophobicity.",
